From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 13:35:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Lessons: the navigation debriefs' figures with the
 turn-on bias held

The debriefs quote the flights' own navigation errors; with the turn-on
bias constant for the flight (previous commit) they are, flown headless
on the lessons' own missions and seeds:

- guid-nav (Falcon 9, 500 m to MECO): MEMS 2 021 m (was 1 662),
  tactical 356 m (337), navigation grade 37 m (38). The debrief said
  about 1.7 km, 0.3 km and a few tens of metres; now about 2 km, 0.4 km
  and a few tens of metres (en, ru, th). Verdicts unchanged: MEMS fails,
  tactical and navigation grade pass. The hint's "a bias of 0.001 g held
  for 150 s alone gives 110 m" is now what the model does, and stays.
- ru-bins-astro (Soyuz-2.1a, 1 000 m to the third stage's cut-off):
  tactical without the star tracker 3 357 m (2 923), with it 1 184 m
  (989), navigation grade 360 m (382), MEMS with the star tracker
  13 018 m (11 000), with GNSS 3 m (3). The debrief said about 3 km and
  0.8 km; now about 3.4 km and 1.2 km (en, ru, th; the pack file
  regenerated with scripts/lesson-packs.ts). The navigation grade's
  "about 0.4 km" and GNSS's "a few metres" stand.

The star tracker flight now fails the lesson's 1 000 m bound (1 184 m),
so tests/heavy/lesson-packs-sixdof.test.ts S2 ("the star tracker ...
keeps it inside") fails. Neither the bound nor the test is changed here:
the bound is a teaching choice, left to the owner (numbers in the
report). A star tracker sees the attitude, not the accelerometers' bias;
a tactical unit's 500 ug held for the 524 s to the cut-off gives
1/2 b t^2 = 0.67 km per axis, about 1.2 km in three, by itself.

